%erg 0.1
Title: The sitter notices its own removal and says so while it still can
Created: 2026-09-12
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-12T14:32Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

The author's observation of 2026-09-12, and it reframes ticket 0727: **when the
sitter evaporates from the Extensions window, it is still resident.** That is
already on 0727's record from the night of 2026-09-06 — "the running instance
kept working from memory throughout; the author could still open and close the
dialog, and the toolbar spinner still animated" — but its consequence was never
drawn.

**The consequence, and it is a hole in this repository's own instrumentation.**
`shutdown()` clears the pulse, removes every toolbar button and closes every
dialog. The author saw the spinner still turning and the dialog still working
AFTER the record and the `.xpi` were gone. So `shutdown()` did not run. The
removal happened without the add-on being told.

Everything ticket 0727 built on 2026-09-12 hangs off that teardown: the death
certificate is written from `shutdown()`'s `finally`, and the ring is parked
there too. **Neither fires in the phenomenon.** The instrumentation has a blind
spot shaped exactly like the defect it was built for, and the only reason it was
not obvious is that no arm has ever reproduced the phenomenon to expose it.

**What can witness it.** Only the still-running scope. An external watcher
(`bench/sitter_watch.py`) sees the record vanish but cannot say what the plugin
was doing; the host does not tell the plugin; and the plugin's own teardown is
never invoked. But the plugin is alive, has a heartbeat, and can ask the host
whether it is still installed. It is the victim and the only available witness.

## What to build

A self-check on the existing heartbeat (`heartbeatTick`, 60 s), which asks
`AddonManager.getAddonByID(ADDON_ID)` — or reads the host's record — and
compares the answer against being alive. If this scope is running and the host
says the add-on is not installed, or not active, that IS the phenomenon, caught
live, from inside.

On detecting it, in this order:

1. **Journal it**, at `error` level, with whatever the host answered. This is
   the record the certificate cannot make, and it reaches Zotero's debug output
   as well as the ring.
2. **Write the certificate**, on the same terms the ruling of 2026-09-12 set:
   only when the diagnostics switch is on. The reason field says so — something
   like `vanished-without-teardown`, distinct from every `BOOTSTRAP_REASONS`
   value, because it is not a reason the host gave.
3. **Tell the user, in the window and once.** Plainly: Zotero has removed the
   indexing assistant, indexing has stopped, the work already done is not lost,
   and it will have to be installed again. An apology is appropriate — the user
   did nothing and lost a tool.
4. **Offer the trace.** The diagnostics layer already composes a redacted
   report and copies it to the clipboard (`composeSDTJournalReport`, the
   journal-copy button). Reuse that, do not invent a second path.
5. **Stop pretending to work.** The spinner still turning is itself a small
   lie: the add-on is gone and nothing it starts now will survive. Disarm the
   sweep, leave the window and its message readable.

## Design notes, and one boundary that needs a ruling

- **"Send a trace" must mean copy, not upload.** SPEC.md R10 counts exactly two
  opt-in egress paths and §6 now discloses a third thing the host does on its
  own. A network send from the plugin would be a new egress path and is NOT in
  scope here without the author's ruling. What is in scope is making the trace
  one click to copy and telling the user where to paste it.
- **This must not fire on an ordinary uninstall.** A user who removes the add-on
  on purpose gets `shutdown('uninstall')` first, and the ruling of 2026-09-12
  says that path leaves cleanly and silently. The self-check has to be disarmed
  by the teardown it is meant to detect the absence of — the `sealed` flag and
  the generation token are the existing machinery for that.
- **Nor on a disable.** `shutdown('disable')` runs, so the same guard covers it.
- The check costs one `AddonManager` call a minute. The heartbeat already runs
  at that cadence and already journals.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `heartbeatTick` (the cadence),
  `writeSDTDeathCertificate` (the file and its gate), `shutdown` (what must
  disarm this), `composeSDTJournalReport` and the journal-copy button (the
  trace), the dialog's switch line (where the message goes)
- `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs` — the mock host; the arms below live here
- `verification/probes/sdt_sitter_bootstrap_mutants.py` — one mutant per half
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/RELEASE-NOTES.md` — what the user is being promised
- `tickets/0727-…erg` — the parent; this closes its blind spot, not its cause

## Test

Red first, and the pairing matters more than the count:

- A scope that is alive while the host says the add-on is gone journals it,
  writes the certificate with the switch on, shows the message once, and
  disarms the sweep.
- **The control:** an ordinary `shutdown('uninstall')` followed by the host
  saying the add-on is gone journals NOTHING and shows no message. Without this
  arm the feature would fire on every deliberate removal, which is both wrong
  and the loudest possible way to be wrong.
- A `shutdown('disable')` likewise.
- The message appears once, not once a minute.
- With the switch off, the message still appears and no file is written.
- A host whose `getAddonByID` throws leaves the render loop and the sweep
  running, journalling the failure — the guard every host read in this file has.

## Invariants

- Nothing here adds an egress path. The trace is copied, never sent.
- The ruling of 2026-09-12 stands: a deliberate uninstall stays clean and
  silent, and this feature must be invisible on that path.
- The heartbeat must not acquire a throw from this check.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The self-check exists, on the heartbeat, with every arm above green and
      each seen red once.
- [ ] The user-facing message and the copy-the-trace offer are in the window.
- [ ] `writeSDTDeathCertificate` accepts the `vanished-without-teardown` reason
      and the gate still holds.
- [ ] Mutants added; the gate catches each.
- [ ] 0727's log records that its teardown-based instrumentation had this blind
      spot and that this ticket closes it.
